From: minsky@media.mit.edu (Marvin Minsky)
Date: Mon, 19 Sep 94 22:05:18 -0400
Subject: [#94-9-293] what's next?

>At 11:44 AM 9/19/94 -0700, Robin Hanson wrote:
>>No matter how suddenly a breakthrough may happen in a
>>laboratory, if the breakthrough requires changes in consumer and
>>organizational habits and styles, it will just take a lot of time to
>>come into wide use.
>
>Yes, indeed. Economists are still waiting for personal computers to have a
>measurable effect on productivity. Electricity took (in the range of) 40
>years.
>
>Dave

Economists are interested mainly in annual interest return on investment.
They can't deal with 40 year intervals.  A research domain can have, say
minus 2 percent per year return for 30 years and then have 25 percent
positive annual return for the next few decades--wiping out the long term
loss in two or three years.

Please don't ask what the negative interest rate was charged to.
Syntactically, it sounded good.

WHy can't they deal with longer intervals?  Because they've grown
accustomed to such short lives, as we can see by how little has been
invested in cryonics and similar research.



