From: minsky@media.mit.edu (Marvin Minsky)
Date: Mon, 19 Sep 94 23:58:23 -0400
Subject: [#94-9-297] what's next?

>At 10:05 PM 9/19/94 -0400, Marvin Minsky wrote:
>>Economists are interested mainly in annual interest return on investment.
>>They can't deal with 40 year intervals.
>
>Actually, they're interested in present value of total return, as a rule.


Yes, I guess so--but in my few conversations with them, they generally lose
interest in things that might take 40 years.  Like, in particular
nanotechnology, which I think may become the largest industry--but not soon
enough for a wealthy individual to profit much from in a present lifetime.

And now, looking at what I just wrote, I see that, indeed, I'm probably


>...accountants? Investors? Anyhow, in this particular
>case,  the conundrum economists seem interested in is "Gee, electric light
>and power seem like a really great things. Why did it take them 40 years to
>start making people appreciably richer?", not "Was research in electricity
>a good investment?"

Well, yes, those are nice puzzles--and I find similar problems within
science itself.  One question that bothers me is why psychology took so
long to develop.  In particular, those experiments of Piaget with children
and water jars.  If you explain what Piaget found to parents, they usually
don't believe you.  They'll say "My five year old would not make *that*
mistake." Then they go home and try it.  They report, "Amazing.  I can't
believe the kid is so dumb."  But isn't it strange that this phenomenon (of
children not having the adult idea about conservation of substances) wasn't
discovered until the 1930s.  Why not in the 1830s -- the 1430, or a couple
of thousand years ago?  The needed equipment--jars of water, or just a few
pebbles--was always available.  But, so far as I can see, in all ancient
psychology or philosophy, it is hard to see any trace of the idea that
young children might think differently.  Instead, they're always regarded
as merely ignorant adults.



